Apparently, those files are coming from an ancient upgrade in the 3.2x
era. First version installed has been 3.2C (if my memory serves well).
It has been upgraded several times. It seems that it's safe to remove
them. They (the files) haven't been making troubles, it's just nice to
know what's what on your disks.

Dr. Tom Blinn <tpb@zk3.dec.com> wrote:
> They were created by one of the older installupdate procedures, maybe when you
> were updating between some pair of the V3.2x releases.  You can delete them.
> 
> They are copies of the named files that were active at the time of the update,
> which were moved aside (and should eventually have been deleted) during the
> update process, so that the running processes would not have thier executable
> files and libraries deleted out from under them.

Yaacov Fenster <fenster@zk3.dec.com> wrote:
> It seems that you upgraded from 3.2C to one of 3.2{D,E,F,G}. While
> upgrading "protected"
> files that are likely to be in use during the upgrade (like libc.so) a
> copy of the "active"
> file is saved off to the side. Based upon the date of the files they
> seem like original
> 3.2C ones, and definitly not in use in 4.0B.

